Ebola has killed more than 2,000 people  in the east of the Democratic Republic  of Congo over the last year the UN  secretary-general Antonio Guterres flew  to the epicenter of the outbreak  the town of Beni to express solidarity  we need to put the same effort into the  fight against Ebola as we have with  dealing with insecurity in all affected  regions by mobilizing all means inside  the country and with the international  community in order to eradicate this  outbreak since the first cases were  identified a year ago the government  struggled to contain it  many people don't trust the government  or its health workers some of the  treatment centers have been attacked by  militia see a problem the control of  Ebola outbreak is taking too much time  this outbreak is serious this is why we  need the government to do more but the  response has been helped by an effective  vaccine secretary-general met people  who've recovered from the virus  treatments are more effective than  before chances of survival are higher  kuvira  Alan a lost most of her family before  she recovered her mother was the first  to die after my mom's burial I also  became terribly ill with a high fever  then they started giving me medicine  when I got better I was welcomed back by  my other relatives in spite of  international support and the improving  treatment new cases keep emerging  further and further from where the  outbreak began Guterres has promised  more support is the world's second worst  Ebola outbreak people are waiting to see  how many more months or years it will  take to bring it under control  Malcolm Webb al-jazeera Nairobi Kenya
 

